  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 234 PM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nThe upper level pattern this afternoon features deep troughing\nover the central CONUS acquiring a slight negative tilt as it\nlifts from the central Plains towards the Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley, with difluent flow out ahead of it over the Upper\nGreat Lakes. At the surface, an elongated trough spans from the\ncentral Plains to Lake Superior, with rain showers ongoing \nalong and ahead of an associated frontal zone from Wisconsin \ninto Upper Michigan. As such, the bulk of precipitation over our\narea today has been confined to the eastern U.P. while the \nnorthern Lower has remained mostly dry. The best precipitation\nchances will remain over the eastern U.P. and northwestern Lower\nthrough into tonight as a surface low tracks from the eastern\nDakotas to western Lake Superior, with locations east of US-131\nstaying mostly dry into Wednesday morning. Expect fairly minimal\nprecipitation amounts through tonight as the best forcing\nresides to the north and west, with a tenth to a quarter inch \nof rain possible for the eastern U.P. and generally less than a\ntenth across the northern Lower.\n\nNorthern Michigan remains in the warm sector through Wednesday,\nallowing for another seasonably warm day with highs in the mid\nto upper 60s across most of the area. Rain looks to spread back\ninto the area from the south and west in the afternoon as a\nsecondary surface low lifts into the Upper MS River Valley and\nswings a front across Lower Michigan. Precipitable water values\nwill remain well above an inch, near the climatological max for\nearly November, into the afternoon. However, precipitation\namounts still look rather limited as the better forcing remains\ndisplaced to the west through most of the day.\n\nShowery weather persists into Thursday as the upper low \nmeanders over the Upper MS River Valley and then phases with a \nnorthern stream shortwave crossing Hudson Bay, finally shifting\nthe trough eastward into the Great Lakes. High temperatures \nwill subsequently drop closer to seasonal norms in the 50s as\n850 mb temps cool close to 0 C over the eastern U.P. and 3-5 C\nover the northern Lower by Thursday evening. Cool and showery\nweather lingers into Friday as the upper low remains over Lower\nMichigan, with continued cold air advection dropping high into\nthe 40s for Friday afternoon. There is some indication of drier\nweather for the weekend as troughing shifts eastward and \nsurface high pressure builds in from the west, allowing temps to\nrebound back into the 50s by Sunday and early next week.",
